(vector (map (:db/id -1)
 (:adr/id "adr-2609100400-murakumo-context-is-the-model-max")
 (:adr/title "murakumo の context は model ごとの最大（Qwen3.8-27B = 262144）。1M は platform 上限で、この重みでは device-lost")
 (:adr/status "accepted")
 (:adr/date "2026-09-10")
 (:adr/tags (vector "murakumo"
"inference"
"context"
"llama.cpp"
"hermes"
"capacity"
"gad"))
 (:adr/related (vector "adr-2609100300-hermes-bots-onto-murakumo-api"
"adr-2608251016-hermes-murakumo-public-api"
"adr-2607173100"
"adr-2608136000"))
 (:adr/body "## Context\n\nOwner instruction (2026-09-10): raise murakumo's context length -- up to 1M,\neach model to its own maximum.\n\nThe hermes fleet had just been cut onto `api.murakumo.cloud` (adr-2609100300)\nand was running only via its OpenRouter fallback, because requests above\nabout 3,000 prompt tokens came back `502 murakumo fleet unreachable:\nconnection_refused` with **no `x-murakumo-route-head` header at all**.\n\n## What was actually wrong\n\nNot the configured window. `cloud-murakumo-api`'s `src/model_token_limits.js`\nalready carried `murakumo-main: { contextWindow: 262144 }`, landed the same\nday in #249 (\"Use the main pool context at token admission\"), with the right\nreasoning written next to it: the pool router excludes heads a request cannot\nfit, so admission must use the widest head's window, not the narrowest.\n\nThe missing head was the point. On gad (`root@100.82.98.110`):\n\n    murakumo-ring.service   enabled, but ActiveState=failed\n                            Result=timeout, ExecMainStatus=9/KILL\n\nIts last **stop** timed out, systemd SIGKILLed it, and `Restart=on-failure`\ndoes not recover a unit that failed while stopping. Nothing restarted it. Its\nExecStart is `-c 524288 --parallel 2 --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8090` -- the port\nthe Worker's VPC service (`GAD_LLAMA` over tunnel c75a3e83) dials. With it\ndown, nothing listened on gad:8090, the router found no head wide enough for\nanything above b70's 16,384-token slot, and the request matched nothing.\n\n    systemctl reset-failed murakumo-ring && systemctl start murakumo-ring\n\nrestored two 262,144-token slots.\n\n## Measured, before and after\n\n| request | before | after |\n|---|---|---|\n| 3,018 prompt tokens (15,115 body bytes) | HTTP 200 | HTTP 200 |\n| ~3,200 prompt tokens (15,615 body bytes) | **HTTP 502**, no route-head | **HTTP 200** `served-head: gad` |\n| 12,022 prompt tokens (60,000 chars) | HTTP 502 | **HTTP 200** `served-head: gad` |\n\n## 1M is the platform ceiling, not this model's\n\n`PLATFORM_TOKEN_LIMIT = 1_000_000` already exists in\n`src/model_token_limits.js` and already applies to the models that genuinely\nhave a 1M window (`qwen/qwen3.8-flash`, `z-ai/glm-5.3-flash`, publisher\nmetadata). For murakumo's own weights it is not reachable, and this was\nmeasured rather than reasoned about.\n\nProbing gad with `--ctx-size 1048576` on llama.cpp b9334 does **not** get\ncapped the way `infer.edn`'s older note (written for the retired Qwen3.6 MoE)\ndescribes. It warns and then tries:\n\n    W llama_context: n_ctx_seq (1048576) > n_ctx_train (262144)\n                     -- possible training context overflow\n    E llama_init_from_model: failed to initialize the context:\n                     vk::Queue::submit: ErrorDeviceLost\n\nand the device is lost. `--ctx-size 262144 --parallel 1` loads, serves, and\nanswers a real completion; host memory went to 16 GiB of 46.\n\nSo **262,144 is this model's own maximum** -- it is Qwen3.8-27B's\n`n_ctx_train`, it is what gad's slots serve, and above it the weights are\noutside their trained window even where memory allows. That is the number\n\"model ごとの最大 context\" resolves to here. 1M stays as the platform cap.\n\nPer-head windows, read from each head's own `/slots` and `/props`:\n\n    gad      262144 x 2 slots    46 GiB host, Radeon 8060S 73 GiB device\n    b70       16384 x 2 slots    15 GiB host; unit peak 14.4 GiB -- at its ceiling\n    xavier     8192 x 1 slot\n\nb70 is **not** raisable: its own unit records that the box has ~4 GiB free\nand \"a config that needed more memory would not have been admissible at all\",\nand the measured unit peak is 14.4 GiB of 15 GiB. Its 16,384 is a serving-slot\nlimit and correctly stays one.\n\n## Consumer side\n\n`scripts/hermes-murakumo-api.cljs` raises the fleet's `context_length` from\n65536 (hermes's own hard floor, `MINIMUM_CONTEXT_LENGTH = 64_000`) to\n**262144**, the model maximum. `max_tokens` stays 2048: at gad's measured\n3.5-4 tok/s under load, a large output is minutes of a slot.\n\nTwo defects in that script were found by running the fleet, not by reading\nfiles back:\n\n1. **`suji-anatomy` had cron jobs and no `config.yaml`.** The script rewrote\n   only configs that already existed. Hermes lets a NAMED provider carry a\n   `base_url` override only when that provider is DECLARED in the config the\n   job runs under (`tools/cronjob_job_args.py::_validate_cron_base_url`), so\n   with no config the guard failed closed:\n\n       blocked for safety: base_url 'https://api.murakumo.cloud/v1'\n       is not allowed for provider 'murakumo'\n\n   `needs-config?` now also covers a profile with enabled jobs and no config.\n   Verified directly against the guard: ALLOWED for suji-anatomy, cron-health,\n   hyakka, amu, x402-ops.\n\n2. **The script's own head check conflated BUSY with ABSENT.** It required\n   `free > 0` and so reported \"heads >= hermes 64K floor: NONE\" against a pool\n   whose 262144-token head was present, healthy and decoding two real\n   requests. Membership is the question; free slots are reported alongside.\n   That is the same failure this workspace keeps recording, committed by the\n   detector written to catch it.\n\n## The reason nobody noticed\n\n`https://api.murakumo.cloud/ready` answered **`ok: true`** for the entire\noutage, with it demoted to `degraded: [\"fleet-incomplete\",\n\"primary-unavailable\"]` inside the payload. A monitor reading `.ok` saw green\nwhile the primary head was dead. `--verify` in the hermes script therefore\nreports the heads and their windows, not the endpoint's verdict about itself.\n\n## Not done, and named\n\n- **`/ready` returning `ok: true` with a dead primary head is unfixed.** It is\n  in `cloud-murakumo-api`; this session did not change that repo.\n- **`murakumo-ring.service` can strand itself again.** A stop that exceeds\n  `TimeoutStopSec` lands it in `failed`, and `Restart=on-failure` does not\n  recover that state. No watchdog restarts it.\n- `qwen3.8-27b-throughput`, `-5090` and `-fastmtp-aggressive` are absent from\n  the DEFAULTS table and fall to `UNKNOWN` (16384/4096). `-5090` answers 404\n  and `-fastmtp-aggressive` reports `is_ready: false`.\n- The gateway's input reserve counts **UTF-8 bytes as tokens** (+512). That is\n  deliberately conservative and documented as such, but it means the usable\n  window is roughly a quarter of the configured one for English and about a\n  third for Japanese. Unchanged here: it is an admission-safety heuristic and\n  changing it is its own risk decision.\n\n\n## What changed the same day (2026-09-10, owner decision)\n\nThe measurement above stands: 262,144 is Qwen3.8-27B's `n_ctx_train`, 1M is\nunreachable on these weights (`vk::Queue::submit: ErrorDeviceLost`), and\nrestarting `murakumo-ring` did restore two 262,144-token slots.\n\nWhat no longer holds is **where** that happens.\n\nHours after the restart gad crashed again -- `vk::DeviceLostError` thrown\ninside `llama_decode`, `23.7G memory peak, 8.5G memory swap peak` -- while\nserving traffic whose prompts had grown because this session raised hermes's\n`context_length` to 262144. Each restart attempt then leaked VRAM until 41.5\nGiB of 48 was stranded, confirmed against two sources (`rocm-smi` and sysfs\n`mem_info_vram_used`), and ComfyUI could see only 6.4 GiB free.\n\nThe owner's decision was to stop contending for that box: gad is video\ngeneration only, inference runs elsewhere. `fleet.edn` already labelled it\nwith the comfy-render role. See adr-2609100500 for the cutover and its\nmeasured state.\n\nSo `murakumo-ring` on gad is now stopped and disabled deliberately. Do not\nrestart it to fix a context ceiling -- that is the instruction this ADR would\notherwise keep giving after the decision that reversed it.\n")))
